<h2>My Story</h2>
<h2>1. Hello, World!</h2>
<p>
    When I was around 8 years old, I convinced my parents to buy me a book
    that claimed to teach how to create games. Being a big fan of video games
    back then, as I am now, I was instantly fond of the idea of creating
    my very own game. The book was about Scratch, the orange cat platform.
    As much as people tend to mock it for its simplicity, it really
    taught me the fundamentals of logic in programming. I'd go as far as to
    say I wouldn't be as good of a programmer as I am now if it weren't for
    the hours sat in front of our old family Lenovo desktop dragging blocks
    on the screen to make the funny cat move where I wanted it to.
    This was my very first contact with coding and it brought me a long
    way to understanding more complex topics later in my life.
</p>
<h2>2. Caught in the Web</h2>
<p>
    Some time passed and I got bored. That's when I found another programming
    book. This one taught the basics of HTML, CSS, and Javascript. It was
    called something along the lines of "CoderDojo Nano: Create with Code".
    Back then I wasn't really familiar with creating websites on the
    internet. For all I knew, you just had to drag-and-drop some blocks
    on a page and press "publish". Oh boy was I surprised when I first
    opened that book. Regardless of my initial shock, I decided to push
    through and followed the instructions. I wanted to have a website,
    afterall. (note that this was before I had access to social media,
    so having a personal page back then was a huge deal) In no time,
    I had a text editor up and running, slowly typing away at my first
    HTML file, "index.html". Slowly but surely, I went through the book
    implementing everything I saw and then editing the colors and styles
    to my liking. The result was a hilarious green-yellow page with a red border and
    a rainbow wave background. I made a personal "portfolio" that featured
    all my published Scratch games as iframes and a list of my best friends
    in order of my liking. It also featured a page with my personal introduction
    and a list of my favorite things. (note that I was at most 10 years old
    when I did this) Additionally, I wanted to be a
    YouTuber, so I created a promotional page on my website that featured
    my videos and listed all the reasons people should subscribe to my
    channel. Most of this website has sadly been lost to time, but I have
    very fond memories of it. This journey of mine taught me a lot and gave
    me a very good foundation to build my web-dev skills in the future.
</p>
<h2>3. The Serpent</h2>
